    closer: [
      { title: "Loving the Prophet and good people", body: "Doesn't this rule out loving the Prophet, scholars and good people? No. Loving them is praised, because “the messenger of the beloved is beloved, and whoever loves the beloved is beloved.” All of it traces back to the same root rather than competing with it." },
      { title: "Why this love can't be shared out", body: "Anything we love a person for, someone else could also have, so that love is always shared. But no one shares God's qualities, even in possibility. So love of Him is the only love that can be complete and undivided." },
    ],
    distinction: ["Two ways loves can relate", "As rivals", "Loving one thing takes something away from another.", "As branches of one root", "Love for the Prophet, a teacher or a friend traces back to the same root instead of competing with it."],
    misreading: "Don't read this as forbidding love for anyone else. Ghazali praises loves that trace back to God. What he denies is loving something as if it stood on its own.",
    reflection: "Take one thing you are attached to and ask which of the five causes it runs on.",
    audit: ["Which of the five causes is strongest in me?", "Which of my loves trace back to God, and which don't?", "Do I love God more for His gifts or for Himself?", "Which of God's qualities am I trying to grow in?"],
